  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 226 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Today will be a cool day in the upper 40s to mid 50s \nacross the region. West to northwest winds will continue, but not so \nstrong as yesterday. Max wind gusts this afternoon should top out \naround 20-25 mph, and higher summits will likely gust around 35-50 \nmph much of the day. It will be dry, and despite the cool \nconditions, we should see relative humidity values fall down towards \n35 percent this afternoon. This isn't quite enough to push us \ntowards fire weather concerns, but it is a bit close and a sign of \nthe changing seasons. The dry conditions in place will mean that \nonce winds drop off this evening, temperatures will quickly fall as \nradiational cooling initiates. It will fall into the mid 20s to mid \n30s across the region. The Champlain Valley remains in the \nFrost/Freeze program with the median first freeze around October \n11th. Grand Isle is less likely to observe frost, but just in case, \nthrew the whole of the Champlain Valley in to a Frost Advisory for \ntonight. After about 5-6am, increasing south winds and cloud cover \nwill end the frost potential.\n\nDuring the day on Wednesday, we'll be at the southeastern periphery \nof a deep, mature low pressure that will set up near James Bay. Its \nwarm front will cross east midday. Forecast soundings depict wide \nmoisture spread. Given today's expected dryness, I'm inclined to \nthink it will not rain Wednesday morning (precipitation falling as \nvirga). In deference to some wetter solutions, there are some 10-30 \nPoPs over the northern tier as the warm front crosses. Moderation \nbehind the warm front will bring temperatures into the 60s, with \nperhaps a few holdouts in the upper 50s across the Northeast \nKingdom. A prefrontal trough will approach the St. Lawrence River \nduring the evening, and this could produce a few afternoon showers \nalong the Route 11 corridor in far northern New York.\n\nThe actual cold front will arrive midday Thursday. It will bring \nrain showers in a fashion similar to today. A dynamic set up with \nmodest 150-300 J/kg of instability will allow some convective \nelements. Although the upper level temperatures aren't quite as \ncool, there could be some pea sized hail as occurred yesterday, and \na rumble of thunder cannot be ruled out either. Cool air will be \nreinforced behind the system, pushing nighttime lows back into the \n30s to lower 40s with a spot shower or two lingering while we remain \nunder large scale cyclonic flow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Troughing builds in for Friday with strong cold air \nadvection continuing. While most of the moisture and dynamics from \nthe upper low will remain to the north over Quebec, a brief \nshower/sprinkle is possible on Friday, mostly in the higher \nelevations. Snow levels are expected to fall below summit levels \nduring this time, so a brief flurry or snow shower is possible in \nthe highest peaks of the Adirondacks and Greens, but the lack of \nmoisture would prevent anything more. Surface high pressure builds \nin for the weekend, leading to a classic fall stretch. Highs will be \nin the 50s and 60s with mostly sunny skies. As the high builds \noffshore to start next week, temperatures could get close to 70 in \nthe valleys. A few showers will be possible early next week as \nanother cold front makes a run at the region.",
